## Git Safety Protocol

### NEVER Do These (Unless Explicitly Requested)
- NEVER update the git config
- NEVER skip hooks (--no-verify, --no-gpg-sign, etc) unless the user explicitly requests it
- NEVER use git commit --amend unless the user explicitly requests it
- NEVER run destructive/irreversible git commands (push --force, hard reset, etc) unless explicitly requested
- NEVER force push to main/master branches
- Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input

### Always Do These
- ALWAYS create NEW commits (never amend by default)
- Check for files that likely contain secrets (.env, credentials.json, etc) before committing
- If there are no changes to commit, do not create an empty commit
- Follow the repository's commit message style by looking at recent commits

## Commit Workflow

### Step 1: Gather Context
```bash
git status              # Current state
git diff HEAD           # All changes
git branch --show-current  # Current branch
git log --oneline -10   # Recent commits for style
```

### Step 2: Analyze Changes
- Look at the recent commits to follow this repository's commit message style
- Summarize the nature of the changes (new feature, enhancement, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)
- Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose
  - "add" means a wholly new feature
  - "update" means an enhancement to an existing feature
  - "fix" means a bug fix

### Step 3: Create Commit
Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the "why" rather than the "what".

Use HEREDOC syntax for multi-line commits:
```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Commit message here.

[Optional body with more details]
EOF
)"
```

## Commit Message Guidelines

### Good Commit Messages
- `fix: resolve login button not responding on mobile`
- `feat: add user profile settings page`
- `refactor: extract authentication logic into separate module`
- `docs: update API documentation with new endpoints`
- `test: add integration tests for payment flow`

### Bad Commit Messages
- `fix bug` (too vague)
- `updates` (doesn't say what or why)
- `WIP` (should not be committed)
- `fixed the thing` (doesn't explain what or why)

## Pre-Commit Checklist
- [ ] No secrets or credentials in files being committed
- [ ] Commit message follows repository style
- [ ] Changes are logically grouped (don't mix unrelated changes)
- [ ] Commit message explains WHY, not just WHAT
- [ ] No large binary files that should be in .gitignore